  {
    "directory": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14006925",
    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-49277",
      "knowledge_point_id": "7d309fd9-62bd-5307-9dfc-128f9ff028aa",
      "knowledge_point": "Integrating a Trigonometric Expression Using Identities and Substitution",
      "problem": "$\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡x{sec}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=$",
      "worked_solution": "We use the identity $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}$ and rewrite the integral as\n\n$$\n\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡x{sec}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=\\int \\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe can solve this by substitution. Let $u=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$ Then \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,⟹\\,\\text{d}u=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =-\\int \\frac{1}{{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{u}+C \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)^{-1}+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices_complete": true,
          "choices": [
